<body class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-800">

    <div class="container mx-auto p-4 md:p-8 max-w-4xl">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="text-center mb-12">
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900">Lab 11: 🎨 Generative AI Project</h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mt-4">Teaching a computer how to be creative by making two models compete.</p>
            <p class="text-md text-gray-500 mt-2">Libraries: PyTorch, PIL, Matplotlib • Estimated Time: 3 hours</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Section 1: The Concept -->
        <section class="lab-card">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-800">Part 1: The Art Forger and the Detective</h2>
            <p>Imagine a game between two people: an art forger and an art detective.</p>
            <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2 mt-4">
                <li>The <span class="font-semibold">Forger's</span> job is to create paintings that look exactly like Rembrandts. At first, their forgeries are terrible.</li>
                <li>The <span class="font-semibold">Detective's</span> job is to look at a painting and decide if it's a real Rembrandt or a fake. At first, they aren't very good at it.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-4">Now, imagine they play this game for months. The forger shows a fake painting to the detective. The detective says "Fake! The brushstrokes are all wrong." The forger learns from this feedback and makes a better forgery next time. But as the forger gets better, the detective also gets better at spotting even tiny imperfections. This competition forces both of them to become world-class experts.</p>
            <div class="info-box">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">This is a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)!</h4>
                <p>A GAN is just this game, but with two neural networks:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc ml-5 mt-2">
                    <li><strong>The Generator (Forger):</strong> Takes random noise as input and tries to generate realistic-looking images.</li>
                    <li><strong>The Discriminator (Detective):</strong> Takes an image (real or fake) and tries to classify it as real (1) or fake (0).</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mt-2">By training them against each other ("adversarially"), the Generator learns to create incredibly realistic images.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2: Building the GAN in PyTorch -->
        <section class="lab-card">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-800">Part 2: Building the Competitors</h2>
            <p>Today we'll be using <span class="font-semibold">PyTorch</span>, another major deep learning framework alongside TensorFlow. It's especially popular in research and known for its flexibility.</p>
            
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Step 1: Setup and Data Loading</h3>
            <p>First, let's install PyTorch and load the MNIST dataset of handwritten digits, which our Generator will learn to forge.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torch<br>
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torch.nn <span class="keyword">as</span> nn<br>
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torch.optim <span class="keyword">as</span> optim<br>
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torchvision<br>
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torchvision.datasets <span class="keyword">as</span> datasets<br>
                <span class="keyword">import</span> torchvision.transforms <span class="keyword">as</span> transforms<br>
                <span class="keyword">from</span> torch.utils.data <span class="keyword">import</span> DataLoader<br>
                <span class="keyword">from</span> torch.utils.tensorboard <span class="keyword">import</span> SummaryWriter<br><br>

                <span class="comment"># Define transformations: Normalize the images to [-1, 1]</span><br>
                transform = transforms.Compose([<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;transforms.ToTensor(),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;transforms.Normalize((<span class="number">0.5</span>,), (<span class="number">0.5</span>,)),<br>
                ])<br><br>

                <span class="comment"># Load the dataset</span><br>
                dataset = datasets.MNIST(root=<span class="string">"dataset/"</span>, transform=transform, download=<span class="keyword">True</span>)<br>
                loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=<span class="number">64</span>, shuffle=<span class="keyword">True</span>)<br>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Step 2: The Discriminator (The Detective)</h3>
            <p>Our detective is a simple classifier. It takes a flattened 28x28 image (784 pixels) and outputs a single number—the probability that the image is real.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
                <span class="keyword">class</span> Discriminator(nn.Module):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">def</span> __init__(self, in_features):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">super</span>().__init__()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;self.disc = nn.Sequential(<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Linear(in_features, <span class="number">128</span>),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.LeakyReLU(<span class="number">0.1</span>),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Linear(<span class="number">128</span>, <span class="number">1</span>),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Sigmoid(),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;)<br><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">def</span> forward(self, x):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">return</span> self.disc(x)<br>
            </div>
             <div class="your-turn-box">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">💡 Your Turn: Make the Detective Smarter</h4>
                <p>A deeper network might be a better detective. Add another `nn.Linear` layer and `nn.LeakyReLU` activation between the existing two layers. A common size would be `nn.Linear(128, 256)` followed by `nn.Linear(256, 128)`.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Step 3: The Generator (The Forger)</h3>
            <p>Our forger takes a random "inspiration" (a vector of random numbers called the noise or latent vector) and transforms it into something that looks like a 28x28 image.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
                <span class="keyword">class</span> Generator(nn.Module):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">def</span> __init__(self, z_dim, img_dim):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">super</span>().__init__()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;self.gen = nn.Sequential(<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Linear(z_dim, <span class="number">256</span>),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.LeakyReLU(<span class="number">0.1</span>),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Linear(<span class="number">256</span>, img_dim),<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nn.Tanh(),  <span class="comment"># Tanh outputs between -1 and 1, matching our normalized images</span><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;)<br><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">def</span> forward(self, x):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">return</span> self.gen(x)<br>
            </div>
             <div class="your-turn-box">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">💡 Your Turn: Change the Inspiration</h4>
                <p>The `z_dim` is the size of the random noise vector we give the generator. It's like changing the size of the canvas the forger has to work with. How would you change the code if you wanted to use a `z_dim` of `128` instead of `64`? (You'll define this in the next step).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 3: The Training Loop -->
        <section class="lab-card">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-800">Part 3: The Adversarial Training</h2>
            <p>This is where the magic happens. We set up the game and let the networks compete for many rounds (epochs).</p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <span class="comment"># Hyperparameters</span><br>
                device = <span class="string">"cuda"</span> <span class="keyword">if</span> torch.cuda.is_available() <span class="keyword">else</span> <span class="string">"cpu"</span><br>
                lr = <span class="number">3e-4</span><br>
                z_dim = <span class="number">64</span><br>
                image_dim = <span class="number">28</span> * <span class="number">28</span> * <span class="number">1</span>  <span class="comment"># 784</span><br>
                batch_size = <span class="number">32</span><br>
                num_epochs = <span class="number">50</span><br><br>

                <span class="comment"># Initialize networks</span><br>
                disc = Discriminator(image_dim).to(device)<br>
                gen = Generator(z_dim, image_dim).to(device)<br>
                fixed_noise = torch.randn((batch_size, z_dim)).to(device)<br><br>

                <span class="comment"># Optimizers and Loss</span><br>
                opt_disc = optim.Adam(disc.parameters(), lr=lr)<br>
                opt_gen = optim.Adam(gen.parameters(), lr=lr)<br>
                criterion = nn.BCELoss() <span class="comment"># Binary Cross Entropy Loss</span><br><br>

                writer_fake = SummaryWriter(f<span class="string">"logs/fake"</span>)<br>
                writer_real = SummaryWriter(f<span class="string">"logs/real"</span>)<br>
                step = <span class="number">0</span><br><br>

                <span class="keyword">for</span> epoch <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="keyword">range</span>(num_epochs):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">for</span> batch_idx, (real, _) <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="keyword">enumerate</span>(loader):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;real = real.view(-<span class="number">1</span>, <span class="number">784</span>).to(device)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;batch_size = real.shape[<span class="number">0</span>]<br><br>

                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="comment">### Train Discriminator: max log(D(x)) + log(1 - D(G(z)))</span><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;noise = torch.randn(batch_size, z_dim).to(device)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;fake = gen(noise)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;disc_real = disc(real).view(-<span class="number">1</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossD_real = criterion(disc_real, torch.ones_like(disc_real))<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;disc_fake = disc(fake.detach()).view(-<span class="number">1</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossD_fake = criterion(disc_fake, torch.zeros_like(disc_fake))<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossD = (lossD_real + lossD_fake) / <span class="number">2</span><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;disc.zero_grad()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossD.backward()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;opt_disc.step()<br><br>

                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="comment">### Train Generator: min log(1 - D(G(z))) <-> max log(D(G(z))</span><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;output = disc(fake).view(-<span class="number">1</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossG = criterion(output, torch.ones_like(output))<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;gen.zero_grad()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lossG.backward()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;opt_gen.step()<br><br>
                
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="comment"># Print losses and visualize</span><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">if</span> batch_idx == <span class="number">0</span>:<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">print</span>(f<span class="string">"Epoch [{epoch}/{num_epochs}] | Loss D: {lossD:.4f}, loss G: {lossG:.4f}"</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">with</span> torch.no_grad():<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;fake = gen(fixed_noise).reshape(-<span class="number">1</span>, <span class="number">1</span>, <span class="number">28</span>, <span class="number">28</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;data = real.reshape(-<span class="number">1</span>, <span class="number">1</span>, <span class="number">28</span>, <span class="number">28</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;img_grid_fake = torchvision.utils.make_grid(fake, normalize=<span class="keyword">True</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;img_grid_real = torchvision.utils.make_grid(data, normalize=<span class="keyword">True</span>)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;writer_fake.add_image(<span class="string">"Mnist Fake Images"</span>, img_grid_fake, global_step=step)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;writer_real.add_image(<span class="string">"Mnist Real Images"</span>, img_grid_real, global_step=step)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;step += <span class="number">1</span><br>
            </div>
             <div class="your-turn-box">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">💡 Your Turn: Watch the Fight in Real-Time</h4>
                <p>The code above saves logs for TensorBoard. After you start training, open TensorBoard in Colab to see the generated images improve over time and to plot the `lossD` and `lossG`. Ideally, you want them to be in a stable equilibrium, not with one overpowering the other. What happens to the generated images if the Generator's loss plummets to near zero?</p>
            </div>
        </section>
        
        <!-- Section 4: Assignment -->
        <section id="assignment" class="lab-card">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-800">Part 4: Your Mission - Generate Fashion</h2>
             <div class="exercise-box">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-xl mb-2">Assignment: Train a Fashion-GAN</h3>
