        </section>

        <!-- Section 5: Bonus - Style Transfer -->
        <section id="style-transfer" class="lab-card">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-800">Part 5: Bonus - Neural Style Transfer</h2>
            <p>Style transfer is another fascinating generative technique. The goal is to take two images—a <span class="font-semibold">content</span> image (like a photo of your dog) and a <span class="font-semibold">style</span> image (like a Van Gogh painting)—and create a new image that has the content of your photo but is painted in the style of Van Gogh.</p>
            <div class="info-box">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">How it Works</h4>
                <p>We use a pre-trained CNN (like VGG19) that already knows how to recognize features. We then define two losses:
                <ul class="list-disc ml-5 mt-2">
                    <li><strong>Content Loss:</strong> Measures how different the high-level features of our generated image are from the content image.</li>
                    <li><strong>Style Loss:</strong> Measures how different the texture, colors, and patterns (the "style") are between our generated image and the style image.</li>
                </ul>
                We then optimize a new image (starting from noise) to minimize both of these losses simultaneously.</p>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-4">Your Challenge:</h4>
            <p>Find a PyTorch implementation of Neural Style Transfer online (there are many excellent tutorials). Your goal is to apply the style of a famous painting to a personal photograph. In a text cell, show the content image, the style image, and your final creation.</p>
        </section>

